# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  FELIX HARROW — Clockmaker
#  Precise, pedantic, sardonic; timekeeping metaphors; deep guilt.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

=== felix.talk
[if $lantern_night] -> felix.lantern
[if not $met_felix] -> felix.first
[if $clock_running and not $felix_after_clock] -> felix.after_clock
[if $felix_locked and not $felix_after_locked] -> felix.after_locked
[if $mari_knows_felix and not $felix_after_mari] -> felix.after_mari
-> felix.default

=== felix.first
~ set met_felix
n: *Behind the workshop window, a thin man with brass goggles pushed up on his forehead is glaring at a pocket watch as though it owes him money.*
felix: If you've come about the town clock, it is not broken. It is *resting*. It has been resting for seventeen years. It is very well rested.
wren: I'm the new night clerk.
felix[surprised]: Odile's hired an assistant? Good heavens. The causality violations will double.
felix: Felix Harrow. Clocks, barometers, astrolabes, and the occasional reluctant music box. I keep Hushwater's time. Or I would, if Hushwater kept any.
* What happened to the clock? -> felix.first.clock
* Causality violations? -> felix.first.causality

=== felix.first.clock
felix[sad]: It stopped at 11:52 on the night of the Long Storm. Every gear is perfect. Every spring is wound. I've rebuilt the escapement four times.
felix: It simply... won't. As if it's waiting for something.
felix[thinking]: Clocks don't wait. Clocks are the opposite of waiting. That's rather the point of them.

=== felix.first.causality
felix: Letters from the future, Wren. Odile's Chute. It's a violation of every law I hold dear.
felix[thinking]: I've asked to inspect it. She says I may, the moment I stop being "a pompous cog". Negotiations are ongoing.

=== felix.default
[if $clock_running and not $clock_backward] felix[happy]: Listen. No — *listen*. Tick. Tick. Tick. Have you ever heard anything so beautiful?
[if $clock_backward] felix[thinking]: It's running backwards. Seventeen years of perfect engineering and it runs *backwards*. June says it's her father, being funny. I've decided not to argue with her.
[if not $clock_running] felix: The escapement is flawless. The mainspring is wound. It should go. It won't go. Do you know how that feels, Wren?
* How do clocks work, anyway? -> felix.clocks
* Why do you stay in Hushwater? -> felix.stay
* I'll let you work. -> END

=== felix.clocks
felix[happy]: Ah! At last, an intelligent question. A clock is simply a controlled fall. The weight wants to drop; the escapement lets it fall one tooth at a time. Tick. Tick.
felix[sad]: Every clock in the world is just something falling, very slowly, and very politely.

=== felix.stay
felix: Somebody has to fix the clock.
felix[sad]: And somebody has to... be here. When it starts. If it starts. I owe it that.

=== felix.after_clock
~ set felix_after_clock
felix[happy]: Wren! Wren. Listen. Do you hear that?
n: *Above you, the town clock ticks — loud and slow and steady, like a heart that has remembered what it's for.*
felix[happy]: Eleven fifty-three. Then fifty-four. Then fifty-five. I've been standing here listening to it like a fool for an hour.
[if not $clock_backward] felix[sad]: Tobias sat with me. All night. Didn't say a word. Best conversation I've ever had.

=== felix.after_locked
~ set felix_after_locked
felix: I'd rather not discuss the... letter. It's in a drawer. Drawers are excellent inventions. Better than clocks, some days.

=== felix.after_mari
~ set felix_after_mari
felix[sad]: Mari left a bun on my step. With a note. "Not yet. But someday." In flour.
felix: I haven't eaten it. I'm keeping it. Which is absurd. It's a bun.

=== felix.lantern
[if $finale] -> felix.finale
[if $clock_running] felix: I'm staying with the clock tonight. If it chimes midnight — when it chimes midnight — I want to be here.
[if not $clock_running] felix[sad]: Lantern Night. I'll be in the tower. I always am. I watch the harbour from the clock room. I don't go down.

=== felix.finale
felix: Lanterns. Hm. Very inexact. Lovely, though.

# ─── Ask about clues ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

=== felix.ask.c6_typed
felix[thinking]: A high-striking 'e'? That's a worn type-bar. Common in old Remingtons. Mine does it, actually. Annoys me terribly.
~ clue c6_typewriter

=== felix.ask.c6_typewriter
felix: My typewriter is a 1949 Remington Rand, and its 'e' strikes high, and I will not be taking questions about it.

=== felix.ask.c6_1152
felix[sad]: 11:52. I know exactly what minute it is, Wren. I have known it for seventeen years.

=== felix.ask.c6_clockroom
felix[angry]: The clock room is private. Nobody goes in the clock room.
felix[sad]: ...Nobody but me.

=== felix.ask.c6_marguerite
felix[sad]: Daniel Fenn was my friend. We were going to build a boat together. A little one. For June.
n: *He turns back to the watch, but his hands don't move.*

=== felix.ask.c6_memorial
-> felix.ask.c6_marguerite

=== felix.ask.c6_daniel
-> felix.ask.c6_marguerite

=== felix.ask.c6_felix_shift
felix: ...
felix[angry]: Who told you that? Bram. Bram and his great flapping mouth.
felix[sad]: I had a fever. That's all. I had a fever, and Daniel went instead. That's all there is to it.
n: *He says it the way you'd say a line you've rehearsed a thousand times.*

=== felix.ask.c6_dark
felix[sad]: The lighthouse went dark at 11:40. I saw it from the clock room. Twelve minutes later...
felix: I stopped looking at the harbour after that.

=== felix.ask.c6_date
felix: Fourteenth of October, two thousand and nine. You needn't say it aloud. Some dates have their own weather.

=== felix.ask.c6_logbook
felix[surprised]: Tobias *knew*? He — Daniel told him? He's known all this time?
felix[sad]: Seventeen years I've crossed the square to avoid that man. Seventeen years.

=== felix.ask.c6_tobias_guilt
felix[sad]: Tobias blames himself for the lamp. Of course he does. He's a Fenn. They'd blame themselves for the tide.

=== felix.ask.c3_stargazer
felix[happy]: Stargazer? Well, I *am* something of a student of the heavens. I set every clock in this town by sidereal time.
felix[thinking]: Though "stargazer" rather suggests someone who looks at stars for the pleasure of it. I look at them for accuracy.
~ clue c3_felix_scope

=== felix.ask.c3_felix_scope
felix: An 1888 Grubb refractor. Four-inch objective. I use it for transit timing. June Fenn calls it "the old man's spyglass". The impertinence.

=== felix.ask.c3_grandpa
felix: My grandfather? Died in 1979, a clockmaker. Never kept a lamp in his life. Hated lamps. Said they made the shadows lie about the time.

=== felix.ask.c3_granddaughter
felix: June Fenn is Tobias's granddaughter, yes. Daniel's girl. She has his... she has his way of standing.

=== felix.ask.c4_keeper
felix: A keeper? I keep time. Badly, lately. But not lighthouses. Tobias keeps the lighthouse. Tobias keeps a great many things. Grudges, mostly.

=== felix.ask.c3_postmark
felix[angry]: Twenty forty-six! You see? You see? A letter from twenty years hence. A flagrant violation. I should very much like to examine it.

=== felix.ask.c4_date
-> felix.ask.c3_postmark

=== felix.ask.c34_same_hand
felix[thinking]: One author, one date, two incompatible histories. That's not a paradox, Wren. That's a *choice*. Paradoxes are much more polite.

=== felix.ask.c1_cinnamon
felix: Cinnamon? Mari's loaves. Four o'clock. I set my watch by them, since I cannot set it by the town clock.

=== felix.ask.c1_ferry
felix: The 8:15 arrives at 8:21, on average. Bram has never once been punctual. I keep a chart.

=== felix.ask.c5_wheel
felix: The man at the wheel is Bram Calloway, who steers by "feel", which is to say, by guesswork and singing.

=== felix.ask.c2_lamp
felix[sad]: The lamp. Hm. I avoid looking at it, if I can.

=== felix.ask.default
[if pick(3) == 0] felix: I'm afraid that falls outside my expertise, which is clocks, and the precise measurement of disappointment.
[if pick(3) == 1] felix[thinking]: No. Nothing. Is that all? I have a mainspring that won't behave.
[if pick(3) == 2] felix: Hm. Ask Odile. She keeps track of everyone. It's a sort of clockwork, I suppose. Made of gossip.
